Lab Infrastructure
You will work with two machines:

idm-server.example.com (Primary IdM Server - 192.168.1.10)
idm-replica.example.com (Replica Server - 192.168.1.20)
Both machines are running Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 or CentOS Stream 9 with necessary network connectivity already established.

Task 1: Preparing the Replica Server Environment
Subtask 1.1: Verify System Requirements
First, let's ensure our replica server meets all requirements for IdM installation.

Connect to the replica server:
ssh root@idm-replica.example.com
Check system resources:
# Verify memory (minimum 2GB recommended)
free -h

# Check disk space (minimum 10GB free)
df -h /

# Verify CPU cores
nproc
Update the system:
dnf update -y
Subtask 1.2: Configure Network and Hostname
Set the correct hostname:
hostnamectl set-hostname idm-replica.example.com
Verify hostname resolution:
# Check if hostname resolves correctly
nslookup idm-replica.example.com

# Verify reverse DNS lookup
nslookup 192.168.1.20
Configure /etc/hosts if DNS is not properly configured:
echo "192.168.1.10 idm-server.example.com idm-server" >> /etc/hosts
echo "192.168.1.20 idm-replica.example.com idm-replica" >> /etc/hosts
Subtask 1.3: Configure Firewall Rules
Install and start firewalld:
dnf install -y firewalld
systemctl enable --now firewalld
Open required ports for IdM replica:
# Add freeipa-ldap and freeipa-ldaps services
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldap
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=freeipa-ldaps

# Add DNS service
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=dns

# Add Kerberos services
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=kerberos

# Add HTTP and HTTPS
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=http
firewall-cmd --permanent --add-service=https

# Reload firewall configuration
firewall-cmd --reload
Verify firewall rules:
firewall-cmd --list-all
Task 2: Installing the IdM Replica
Subtask 2.1: Install Required Packages
Install IdM client packages first:
dnf install -y ipa-client
Install IdM server packages:
dnf install -y ipa-server ipa-server-dns
Verify package installation:
rpm -qa | grep ipa-server
Subtask 2.2: Prepare Replica Installation File
Before installing the replica, we need to prepare a replica file from the primary server.

Connect to the primary IdM server:
ssh root@idm-server.example.com
Authenticate as admin user:
kinit admin
Prepare the replica installation file:
ipa-replica-prepare idm-replica.example.com --ip-address=192.168.1.20
Locate the generated replica file:
ls -la /var/lib/ipa/replica-info-idm-replica.example.com.gpg
Copy the replica file to the replica server:
scp /var/lib/ipa/replica-info-idm-replica.example.com.gpg root@idm-replica.example.com:/tmp/
Subtask 2.3: Install the IdM Replica
Return to the replica server:
ssh root@idm-replica.example.com
Install the replica using the prepared file:
ipa-replica-install /tmp/replica-info-idm-replica.example.com.gpg \
  --setup-dns \
  --forwarder=8.8.8.8 \
  --forwarder=8.8.4.4 \
  --no-ntp
Note: The installation process may take 10-15 minutes. You'll be prompted for the Directory Manager password during installation.

Monitor the installation progress:
The installer will display progress information. Key steps include:

Configuring directory server
Setting up Kerberos KDC
Configuring Apache web server
Setting up DNS server
Starting services
Subtask 2.4: Verify Replica Installation
Check IdM services status:
ipactl status
Verify replica is properly joined:
ipa-replica-conncheck --master idm-server.example.com
Test Kerberos authentication:
kinit admin
klist
Task 3: Configuring Replication Between Servers
Subtask 3.1: Verify Replication Topology
Check current replication topology:
ipa topologysegment-find domain
View replication agreements:
ipa-replica-manage list
Check replication status from primary server:
ssh root@idm-server.example.com
kinit admin
ipa-replica-manage list
Subtask 3.2: Configure Additional Replication Settings
Set replication refresh interval (if needed):
# Connect to primary server
ssh root@idm-server.example.com
kinit admin

# Modify replication settings
ipa-replica-manage set-renewal-master idm-server.example.com
Configure replication cleanup:
# Set replication cleanup interval
ipa-replica-manage clean-ruv --force
Subtask 3.3: Test Replication Functionality
Create a test user on the primary server:
ssh root@idm-server.example.com
kinit admin

# Create test user
ipa user-add testuser --first=Test --last=User --password
Verify user replication on replica server:
ssh root@idm-replica.example.com
kinit admin

# Check if user exists on replica
ipa user-show testuser
Create a test group on replica server:
# Still on replica server
ipa group-add testgroup --desc="Test replication group"
Verify group replication on primary server:
ssh root@idm-server.example.com
kinit admin

# Check if group exists on primary
ipa group-show testgroup
Task 4: Verifying Replication Status
Subtask 4.1: Monitor Replication Health
Check replication status using built-in tools:
# On either server
ipa-replica-manage list -v
View detailed replication information:
# Check replication agreements
ipa-csreplica-manage list

# View topology segments
ipa topologysegment-find domain --all
Monitor replication lag:
# Check for replication delays
ipa-replica-manage list-ruv
Subtask 4.2: Verify Service Availability
Test web interface access:
# Test primary server web interface
curl -k https://idm-server.example.com/ipa/ui/

# Test replica server web interface
curl -k https://idm-replica.example.com/ipa/ui/
Verify LDAP connectivity:
# Test LDAP on primary
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://idm-server.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" "(objectclass=*)" dn

# Test LDAP on replica
ldapsearch -x -H ldap://idm-replica.example.com -b "dc=example,dc=com" "(objectclass=*)" dn
Test Kerberos functionality:
# Test kinit on both servers
kinit admin@EXAMPLE.COM
klist
Subtask 4.3: Performance and Monitoring
Check system resources on both servers:
# Monitor CPU and memory usage
top -n 1

# Check disk I/O
iostat -x 1 3

# Monitor network connectivity
ss -tuln | grep -E ':(389|636|88|464|53|80|443)'
Review IdM logs for any issues:
# Check directory server logs
tail -f /var/log/dirsrv/slapd-EXAMPLE-COM/access

# Check IdM installation logs
tail -f /var/log/ipaserver-install.log

# Check system logs
journalctl -u ipa -f
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue 1: Replica Installation Fails
Symptoms: Installation stops with DNS or connectivity errors

Solution:

# Verify DNS resolution
nslookup idm-server.example.com
nslookup idm-replica.example.com

# Check network connectivity
ping idm-server.example.com
telnet idm-server.example.com 389

# Verify firewall rules
firewall-cmd --list-all
Issue 2: Replication Not Working
Symptoms: Changes don't replicate between servers

Solution:

# Check replication status
ipa-replica-manage list -v

# Restart IdM services
ipactl restart

# Force replication sync
ipa-replica-manage force-sync --from idm-server.example.com
Issue 3: Authentication Issues
Symptoms: Cannot authenticate to replica server

Solution:

# Verify Kerberos configuration
klist -k /etc/krb5.keytab

# Check time synchronization
chrony sources -v

# Restart authentication services
systemctl restart krb5kdc
systemctl restart kadmin
Best Practices for IdM Replication
Security Considerations
Use strong passwords for Directory Manager and admin accounts
Implement proper firewall rules to restrict access
Regularly update IdM packages for security patches
Monitor replication logs for suspicious activities
Performance Optimization
Ensure adequate system resources (CPU, RAM, disk)
Use fast storage for directory server databases
Implement proper network bandwidth between replicas
Regular maintenance and cleanup of old replication data
Monitoring and Maintenance
Set up automated monitoring for replication health
Implement backup strategies for both servers
Regular testing of failover scenarios
Documentation of replication topology and procedures
